\label{ch:memory-models}

The memory model defines the contract between a program and its execution environment regarding how memory operations (loads, stores, atomics) behave. A compiler's IR must represent memory operations precisely enough to enable optimization while faithfully preserving the semantics required by the source language and the target hardware. This chapter explores how IRs represent memory, from explicit memory SSA to alias analysis, and how they bridge the gap between high-level language memory models and hardware memory models.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 60.1
%% ============================================================
\section{Memory as Explicit Values (Memory SSA)}\label{sec:memory-ssa}

Memory SSA treats the entire memory state as a virtual value that flows through the program. Each memory operation takes the current memory state as input and produces a new memory state.

\subsection{Memory States}\label{sec:memory-states}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Memory SSA:
  mem0 = initial_memory
  mem1 = store(mem0, ptr_a, value_1)    // write to ptr_a
  mem2 = store(mem1, ptr_b, value_2)    // write to ptr_b
  val = load(mem2, ptr_a)               // read from ptr_a (returns value_1)
\end{verbatim}

Each memory operation is explicitly ordered by its memory state input. The memory state at any point represents the cumulative effect of all preceding memory operations.

\subsection{Memory Phi Functions}\label{sec:memory-phi}

At control-flow join points, memory states are merged with phi functions:

\begin{verbatim}
  B1: mem1 = store(mem0, ptr, v1); goto B3
  B2: mem2 = store(mem0, ptr, v2); goto B3
  B3: mem3 = phi(mem1, mem2)  // memory state depends on path
      val = load(mem3, ptr)   // val is v1 or v2 depending on path
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Advantages of Memory SSA}\label{sec:memory-ssa-advantages}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Explicit dependencies.} Memory dependencies are represented as data dependencies on the memory state.
  \item \textbf{SSA benefits.} All SSA optimizations (CSE, DCE, code motion) apply to memory operations.
  \item \textbf{Alias analysis integration.} The memory state can be split by alias analysis to reduce unnecessary dependencies.
\end{itemize}

