\section{Alias Analysis in IR}\label{sec:alias-analysis}

Alias analysis determines whether two pointers may refer to the same memory location. It is essential for optimizing memory operations.

\subsection{Alias Query}\label{sec:alias-query}

\begin{definition}[Alias Query]\label{def:alias-query}
Given two memory accesses $a_1$ and $a_2$, alias analysis returns one of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{NoAlias.} $a_1$ and $a_2$ definitely access different locations.
  \item \textbf{MayAlias.} $a_1$ and $a_2$ may access the same location.
  \item \textbf{MustAlias.} $a_1$ and $a_2$ definitely access the same location.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Alias Analysis Techniques}\label{sec:alias-techniques}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Type-based.} Pointers of different types do not alias (strict aliasing rule in C/C++).
  \item \textbf{Scope-based.} Local variables do not alias with each other or with globals.
  \item \textbf{Flow-insensitive.} Andersen's analysis: computes a points-to set for each pointer, ignoring control flow.
  \item \textbf{Flow-sensitive.} Steensgaard's analysis (with merging) or more precise analyses that track points-to information along control-flow paths.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Alias Analysis in LLVM}\label{sec:alias-llvm}

LLVM uses a layered alias analysis framework:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Alias analysis pipeline:
  1. BasicAA: simple rules (type-based, scope-based, GEP analysis)
  2. CFL-AA: context-free language-based analysis (more precise)
  3. TBAA: type-based alias analysis (uses type metadata)
  4. Scoped-noalias: uses scope metadata from source language
\end{verbatim}

