\section{Memory Dependencies and Ordering}\label{sec:memory-dependencies}

Memory dependencies determine which memory operations can be reordered.

\subsection{Dependency Types}\label{sec:dep-types}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Raw (Read-After-Write).} A read depends on a preceding write to the same location.
  \item \textbf{War (Write-After-Read).} A write must not overtake a preceding read to the same location.
  \item \textbf{Waw (Write-After-Write).} Two writes to the same location must maintain their order.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Reordering Rules}\label{sec:reordering-rules}

Two memory operations can be reordered if and only if they do not alias (or if the reordering preserves the semantics for all possible aliasing scenarios).

\begin{verbatim}
  // Can reorder (no alias):
  store ptr_a, v1    // write to a
  store ptr_b, v2    // write to b (different from a)

  // Cannot reorder (may alias):
  store ptr_a, v1    // write to a
  load ptr_b         // read from b (may be same as a)
\end{verbatim}

