\section{Hardware Memory Models}\label{sec:hardware-memory-models}

Modern processors do not guarantee sequential consistency: memory operations may appear to execute in a different order than the program specifies.

\subsection{Sequential Consistency}\label{sec:sequential-consistency}

\begin{definition}[Sequential Consistency]\label{def:seq-cst}
A system is \emph{sequentially consistent} if the result of any execution is the same as if all processors' operations were executed in some sequential order, and each processor's operations appear in the order specified by its program.
\end{definition}

Sequential consistency is the simplest memory model but is not provided by modern hardware (for performance reasons).

\subsection{Relaxed Memory Models}\label{sec:relaxed-models}

Modern architectures provide relaxed memory models:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Hardware memory models}\label{tab:hw-memory-models}
\begin{tabular}{lp{8cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Architecture} & \textbf{Memory Model} \\
\hline
x86-TSO & Total Store Order: stores are not reordered with stores; loads may be reordered with stores \\
ARM & Weakly ordered: all reorderings are permitted unless explicitly prevented \\
POWER & Weakly ordered: similar to ARM, with additional complexities \\
RISC-V & RVWMO: weakly ordered, with explicit fence instructions \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Implications for IR}\label{sec:hw-implications}

The IR must represent enough information for the backend to generate correct code for the target's memory model:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Ordering constraints.} The IR must record which memory operations must be ordered.
  \item \textbf{Fences.} The IR must insert fence instructions where needed.
  \item \textbf{Atomics.} The IR must represent atomic operations with their required ordering guarantees.
\end{itemize}

