\section{Sequential Consistency vs. Relaxed Models}\label{sec:seq-vs-relaxed}

The gap between the source language's memory model (typically sequential consistency) and the hardware's memory model (typically relaxed) must be bridged by the compiler.

\subsection{The Compiler's Role}\label{sec:compiler-role}

The compiler must:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Preserve the source language's memory model semantics.
  \item Generate code that is correct for the target hardware's memory model.
  \item Insert fences and barriers where needed to prevent hardware reordering.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Mapping Source to Hardware}\label{sec:source-to-hardware}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Source (C++11):
  x.store(1, std::memory_order_seq_cst);  // sequentially consistent
  y.store(2, std::memory_order_relaxed);  // relaxed

  // IR:
  store.atomic x, 1, seq_cst    // must not be reordered
  store.atomic y, 2, relaxed    // can be reordered

  // Target (x86):
  mov [x], 1          // x86 TSO: stores are ordered
  mfence              // seq_cst requires a fence on x86
  mov [y], 2          // relaxed: no fence needed

  // Target (ARM):
  str 1, [x]
  dmb ish             // seq_cst requires a barrier on ARM
  str 2, [y]          // relaxed: no barrier needed
\end{verbatim}

