\section{IR Support for Atomics and Fences}\label{sec:atomics-fences}

The IR must provide operations for atomic memory access and synchronization fences.

\subsection{Atomic Operations}\label{sec:atomic-ops}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Atomic load:
  %val = load.atomic i32* %ptr, seq_cst

  // Atomic store:
  store.atomic i32 %val, i32* %ptr, release

  // Atomic read-modify-write:
  %old = atomicrmw add i32* %ptr, 1 seq_cst  // atomic increment

  // Compare-and-swap:
  %result = cmpxchg i32* %ptr, %expected, %desired seq_cst seq_cst
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Memory Ordering}\label{sec:memory-ordering}

Atomic operations carry a memory ordering that specifies their synchronization guarantees:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{relaxed.} No ordering guarantees beyond atomicity.
  \item \textbf{acquire.} Subsequent reads/writes cannot be reordered before this operation.
  \item \textbf{release.} Preceding reads/writes cannot be reordered after this operation.
  \item \textbf{acq\_rel.} Both acquire and release.
  \item \textbf{seq\_cst.} Acquire + release + total ordering with other seq\_cst operations.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Fence Instructions}\label{sec:fences}

Fences enforce ordering between memory operations:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Full fence: no memory operation can cross the fence
  fence seq_cst

  // Acquire fence: subsequent operations cannot move before the fence
  fence acquire

  // Release fence: preceding operations cannot move after the fence
  fence release
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{IR Representation}\label{sec:ir-atomics}

The IR represents atomics and fences as special operations with explicit ordering annotations. The backend lowers these to the appropriate hardware instructions (e.g., \texttt{mfence} on x86, \texttt{dmb} on ARM).

