\begin{historical}
The study of memory models began with Lamport's definition of sequential consistency~\cite{lamport1979}. The discovery that hardware does not provide sequential consistency (for performance) led to the development of relaxed memory models and the associated programming challenges. The C11/C++11 memory model~\cite{boehm2008} was the first to provide a standardized, hardware-independent memory model for a mainstream language. The implementation of these models in compilers (LLVM, GCC) required careful design of the IR to represent atomic operations, fences, and ordering constraints. The ongoing challenge is to balance the programmer's need for simplicity (sequential consistency) with the hardware's need for performance (relaxed models).
\end{historical}

