\section{Advanced Alias Analysis and Hardware Memory Models}\label{sec:alias-hardware-advanced}

\subsection{Points-To Analysis Algorithms}\label{sec:points-to-algorithms}

Points-to analysis determines which memory locations a pointer may refer to. Different algorithms offer different trade-offs between precision and cost.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Points-to analysis algorithms compared}\label{tab:points-to-algorithms}
\begin{tabular}{p{2.5cm}p{2cm}p{2cm}p{2cm}p{3cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Algorithm} & \textbf{Flow?} & \textbf{Context?} & \textbf{Cost} & \textbf{Precision} \\
\hline
Naive & No & No & $O(n)$ & Very low \\
Steensgaard & No & No & $O(n \alpha(n))$ & Low \\
Andersen & No & No & $O(n^3)$ & Medium \\
Cycle-collecting & No & No & $O(n^3)$ & Medium-High \\
Flow-sensitive & Yes & No & $O(n^4)$ & High \\
Context-sensitive & No & Yes & $O(n^3 \cdot c)$ & High \\
Both & Yes & Yes & Exponential & Very high \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Andersen's Points-To Analysis}\label{alg:andersen}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{AndersenAnalysis}{$\mathrm{program}$}
  \State \Comment{Initialize points-to sets}
  \For{each pointer variable $p$}
    \State $\mathrm{pts}(p) \gets \emptyset$
  \EndFor
  \State \Comment{Generate constraints from program}
  \For{each assignment $p = \&x$}
    \State Add constraint: $\{x\} \subseteq \mathrm{pts}(p)$
  \EndFor
  \For{each assignment $p = q$}
    \State Add constraint: $\mathrm{pts}(q) \subseteq \mathrm{pts}(p)$
  \EndFor
  \For{each assignment $*p = q$}
    \State Add constraint: $\forall r \in \mathrm{pts}(p).\, \mathrm{pts}(q) \subseteq \mathrm{pts}(r)$
  \EndFor
  \For{each assignment $p = *q$}
    \State Add constraint: $\forall r \in \mathrm{pts}(q).\, \mathrm{pts}(r) \subseteq \mathrm{pts}(p)$
  \EndFor
  \State \Comment{Solve constraints (least fixed point)}
  \Repeat
    \State $\mathrm{changed} \gets \mathrm{false}$
    \For{each constraint}
      \State Apply constraint; update points-to sets
      \If{any set changed}
        \State $\mathrm{changed} \gets \mathrm{true}$
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
  \Until{$\neg \mathrm{changed}$}
  \State \Return $\mathrm{pts}$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Alias Analysis for Optimization}\label{sec:alias-for-optimization}

Alias analysis results directly enable optimizations:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Before alias analysis:
  *p = 1;
  x = *q;  // must read from memory (p and q may alias)

  // After alias analysis (p and q do NOT alias):
  *p = 1;
  x = *q;  // can use cached value of *q (no alias with p)
  // -> load hoisted before the store

  // After alias analysis (p and q MUST alias):
  *p = 1;
  x = *q;  // x is definitely 1 (p == q)
  // -> replaced with: x = 1
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Hardware Memory Models in Detail}\label{sec:hardware-models-detail}

Different processor architectures provide different memory consistency guarantees:

\begin{verbatim}
  // x86-TSO (Total Store Order):
  // - Loads are not reordered with loads
  // - Stores are not reordered with stores
  // - Loads MAY be reordered with earlier stores (to different locations)
  // - Each processor sees its own writes in order

  // ARM (Weakly ordered):
  // - Loads MAY be reordered with loads
  // - Stores MAY be reordered with stores
  // - Loads MAY be reordered with stores
  // - Explicit barriers (DMB, DSH) needed for ordering

  // RISC-V (RVWMO):
  // - Similar to ARM: weakly ordered
  // - FENCE instruction for ordering
  // - LR/SC for atomic read-modify-write
\end{verbatim}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Memory model reorderings permitted by different architectures}\label{tab:hw-reorderings}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\hline
\textbf{Reordering} & \textbf{x86} & \textbf{ARM} & \textbf{POWER} & \textbf{RISC-V} \\
\hline
Load-Load & No & Yes & Yes & Yes \\
Load-Store & No & Yes & Yes & Yes \\
Store-Store & No & Yes & Yes & Yes \\
Store-Load & Yes & Yes & Yes & Yes \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Mapping Source Memory Model to Hardware}\label{sec:source-to-hw-mapping}

The compiler must insert appropriate fences to bridge the gap between the source language's memory model and the hardware's:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Source (C++11 sequentially consistent):
  x.store(1, seq_cst);  // must be totally ordered
  r1 = y.load(seq_cst);

  // On x86 (TSO):
  mov [x], 1      // store (naturally ordered on x86)
  mfence           // seq_cst fence needed (prevents store-load reordering)
  mov r1, [y]     // load

  // On ARM (weakly ordered):
  str 1, [x]       // store
  dmb ish          // full barrier (prevents all reorderings)
  ldr r1, [y]     // load

  // On x86, relaxed operations need no fences:
  // x.store(1, relaxed);
  mov [x], 1      // no fence needed
  // y.load(relaxed);
  mov r1, [y]     // no fence needed
\end{verbatim}

