\subsection{Optimization Under Memory Models}\label{sec:opt-under-mm}

Certain optimizations that are safe for ordinary memory operations become unsafe under relaxed memory models:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Store forwarding.} Replacing a load with a previously stored value is safe only if no other thread can observe the store.
  \item \textbf{Load elimination.} Removing a redundant load is safe only if no intervening store from another thread can change the value.
  \item \textbf{Speculative loads.} Speculatively loading from a pointer is unsafe if the load can trigger a page fault that is observable by other threads.
  \item \textbf{Store merging.} Merging two consecutive stores to the same location is safe only if no other thread can read the intermediate value.
\end{itemize}

\begin{caution}
The interaction between optimization and memory models is one of the most subtle areas in compiler design. An optimization that is valid under sequential consistency may introduce bugs under relaxed memory models. The compiler must track the memory ordering of each operation and respect the constraints imposed by the source language's memory model.
\end{caution}

