\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-60}

Memory models in IRs must bridge the gap between the source language's memory semantics and the target hardware's memory behavior. Memory SSA makes memory dependencies explicit; alias analysis determines which operations may conflict; and atomic/fence operations represent synchronization. The compiler's role is to preserve the source language's guarantees while generating efficient code for the target hardware.

Litmus tests and formal memory model specifications provide tools for verifying compiler correctness. The ongoing challenge is to enable aggressive optimization while respecting the subtle constraints of relaxed memory models.

For further reading, we recommend the C11/C++11 memory model paper~\cite{boehm2008}, the LLVM atomics documentation~\cite{llvm2024}, and Adve and Boehm's survey of memory models~\cite{adve2010}. The work by Batty et al.~\cite{batty2011} on the formalization of the C11 memory model provides a rigorous foundation for memory model verification.
